/-
# The taper shape inequality, and why the curvature donor cannot pay the near core

§34 (`NearCoreGapSplitOptimization`) reduced the finite near core to one fully explicit
criterion,

    (2J+1) · A · log(|t|+J+4) · 9CΛ²/π²  <  ∫q/2      ⟹   0 < ∫ q·S(t,J) ,

where `Λ` is the taper radius and `C` bounds the curvature `‖(q·cosh(a·))''‖₁` of the
taper–height profiles.  The criterion is a theorem; whether it can ever be *met* was left
open.  This module settles that, negatively, and thereby prunes the route.

The obstruction is a shape inequality that holds for every `C²` compactly supported
taper and involves no zeta data at all:

    ∫ q  ≤  Λ² · ∫ |q''|                                (`integral_le_curvature`)

for `q` supported in `[-Λ, Λ]`.  It is proved from two elementary facts about compactly
supported functions:

* `two_mul_abs_le_integral_abs_deriv` — `2|f(x)| ≤ ‖f'‖₁`, because `f(x)` is both the
  integral of `f'` up to `x` and minus its integral from `x` on;
* `integral_le_of_bound_of_support` — a function supported in `[-Λ,Λ]` and bounded by `B`
  has integral at most `2ΛB`.

Applying the first to `q'` gives `‖q'‖∞ ≤ ‖q''‖₁/2`, hence `‖q'‖₁ ≤ Λ‖q''‖₁`; applying it
to `q` gives `‖q‖∞ ≤ Λ‖q''‖₁/2`, hence `∫q ≤ Λ²‖q''‖₁`.

Since `‖q''‖₁` is exactly the curvature of the taper–height profile at height `0`
(`detWeight_zero`), any curvature constant `C` valid on the whole strip of heights
satisfies `∫q ≤ Λ²C`.  Feeding that back into the criterion:

* `criterion_implies_count_lt` — the criterion forces
  `(2J+1)·A·log(|t|+J+4) < π²/18 ≈ 0.548`;
* `not_criterion_of_unit_local_count` — so it fails as soon as the local counting
  constant reaches `A·log(|t|+J+4) ≥ 1`, which any zero-carrying window already forces;
* `floor_nonpos_of_unit_local_count` — equivalently, the explicit floor `∫q/2 − escape`
  of §34.2 is never positive.

The route is therefore pruned, and pruned *with the reason*: the small-gap pin buys at
most `Λ²‖q''‖₁/2`, while a single large-gap zero already costs `9Λ²‖q''‖₁/π²`, which is
larger.  What survives is stated exactly:

* `gap_split_pos_requires_low_gap_proportion` — the split floor of §33 can be positive
  only if `(4/π²)·highGapMass < lowGapMass`: more than a `4/π² ≈ 0.405` proportion of the
  near multiplicity mass must sit within the gap threshold.  The near core therefore
  needs a *clustering* statement about the zeros within distance `π/(3Λ)` of the target
  ordinate — not a counting bound, not an absolute envelope, and not a sharper form of
  the quadratic-decay donor, whose loss is exactly what this module quantifies.

Nothing here asserts or assumes the Riemann Hypothesis, and nothing weakens
`RiemannHypothesisFor`.  No `sorry`, no `axiom`, no Boolean receipt, no numerical
evidence: the constants `π²/18` and `4/π²` are compared using Mathlib's rational bounds
for `π`.
-/
